1
00:00:02,415 --> 00:00:04,766
GPT has a secret animal.

2
00:00:05,046 --> 00:00:08,286
I get to ask yes or no questions, and I have twenty.

3
00:00:08,567 --> 00:00:13,102
Six questions later: "Yes, it was octopus." Then we swap.

4
00:00:13,383 --> 00:00:17,607
I think of an object, and now GPT has to work out what's in my head.

5
00:00:17,887 --> 00:00:20,815
After seventeen questions it says: stapler.

6
00:00:21,094 --> 00:00:22,966
It was right.

7
00:00:23,247 --> 00:00:27,591
I built this with Claude Code, and the narration is AI-generated.

8
00:00:28,391 --> 00:00:33,575
Twenty questions is a nice test for language models because it needs two different skills.

9
00:00:33,855 --> 00:00:39,591
When the model is the host, it has to hold a secret and answer honestly without giving it away.

10
00:00:39,871 --> 00:00:44,767
When the model is the guesser, it has to ask questions that actually narrow things down.

11
00:00:45,047 --> 00:00:46,919
The app lets you play both.

12
00:00:47,199 --> 00:00:49,167
In the first mode, you guess.

13
00:00:49,447 --> 00:00:52,999
In the second, you think of something and GPT guesses.

14
00:00:53,279 --> 00:01:02,087
Each question is about one tiny call to GPT-4o on Azure, with a limit of about sixty tokens, so a game costs very little.

15
00:01:02,887 --> 00:01:05,119
The secret never goes to the player.

16
00:01:05,399 --> 00:01:12,527
It's picked in Python from a list for each category: animals, objects, places, food, tech.

17
00:01:12,807 --> 00:01:15,903
And it lives in the app's memory, not in the chat..

18
00:01:16,183 --> 00:01:25,111
Every question goes to a small helper that asks GPT for strict JSON, at a low temperature of zero point three, so the answers are steady..

19
00:01:25,391 --> 00:01:37,247
The host prompt tells GPT the secret, limits its answers to four words, Yes, No, Sometimes or Irrelevant, and asks it to flag a correct guess in a separate field.

20
00:01:37,527 --> 00:01:41,055
The app, not the model, decides when the game is won.

21
00:01:41,335 --> 00:01:43,951
And the prompt says never reveal the secret.

22
00:01:44,751 --> 00:01:49,263
The category is Animal, and the app picked a secret that I can't see.

23
00:01:49,543 --> 00:01:52,207
My first question: is it bigger than a dog?

24
00:01:52,487 --> 00:01:54,551
The host says: Sometimes.

25
00:01:54,831 --> 00:02:03,159
That's honest, since some of the animals vary a lot in size, but it also tells me a yes or no question about size isn't always clean.

26
00:02:03,439 --> 00:02:05,311
Can it fly?

27
00:02:05,591 --> 00:02:06,335
No.

28
00:02:06,615 --> 00:02:08,487
Does it live in water?

29
00:02:08,767 --> 00:02:10,639
Yes.

30
00:02:10,919 --> 00:02:14,687
So now I'm looking at something that doesn't fly and lives in water.

31
00:02:14,967 --> 00:02:16,839
Does it have feathers?

32
00:02:17,119 --> 00:02:17,863
No.

33
00:02:18,143 --> 00:02:20,015
That rules out the birds.

34
00:02:20,294 --> 00:02:22,167
Does it have eight arms?

35
00:02:22,446 --> 00:02:24,318
Yes.

36
00:02:24,598 --> 00:02:27,094
There aren't many animals with eight arms.

37
00:02:27,374 --> 00:02:29,246
Is it an octopus?

38
00:02:29,526 --> 00:02:31,398
Yes.

39
00:02:31,678 --> 00:02:36,382
The app says: Yes, it was octopus, you got it in six questions.

40
00:02:36,662 --> 00:02:43,454
I should be honest that I wrote this app, so I know roughly what's on the animal list, and that makes it easier for me than for you.

41
00:02:44,254 --> 00:02:46,654
The other direction is harder for the model.

42
00:02:46,934 --> 00:02:51,374
It has no secret to look at, only the history of questions and answers..

43
00:02:51,654 --> 00:02:58,302
Each turn, the app sends the whole history, and GPT replies with either a question or a final guess.

44
00:02:58,582 --> 00:03:03,070
The prompt asks for a smart question that splits the remaining possibilities.

45
00:03:03,350 --> 00:03:05,486
On the last turn it's forced to guess.

46
00:03:05,766 --> 00:03:12,102
The app shows buttons for Yes, No or Sometimes, or Correct and Nope when GPT guesses.

47
00:03:12,902 --> 00:03:14,966
I think of an object on my desk.

48
00:03:15,246 --> 00:03:17,119
I'm not telling you what it is yet.

49
00:03:17,398 --> 00:03:20,518
GPT's first question is: is it a living thing?

50
00:03:20,798 --> 00:03:21,542
No.

51
00:03:21,822 --> 00:03:30,486
The next three questions are good ones: is it man-made, yes; is it larger than a microwave, no; is it an electronic device, no.

52
00:03:30,766 --> 00:03:33,694
Then it asks if it's commonly found in a household.

53
00:03:33,974 --> 00:03:36,158
For a stapler I answered Sometimes.

54
00:03:36,438 --> 00:03:42,846
Then personal care, which is no, entertainment, no, and work or productivity, yes.

55
00:03:43,126 --> 00:03:45,526
Is it a tool for writing or drawing?

56
00:03:45,806 --> 00:03:46,550
No.

57
00:03:46,830 --> 00:03:47,622
Cooking?

58
00:03:47,902 --> 00:03:48,646
No.

59
00:03:48,926 --> 00:03:50,798
An office or workspace?

60
00:03:51,078 --> 00:03:52,950
Yes.

61
00:03:53,230 --> 00:03:55,438
Used for organizing or storing?

62
00:03:55,718 --> 00:03:57,590
Sometimes.

63
00:03:57,870 --> 00:03:59,742
Made of plastic?

64
00:04:00,022 --> 00:04:04,054
Sometimes, since a stapler can be plastic, metal or both.

65
00:04:04,334 --> 00:04:06,206
On a desk?

66
00:04:06,486 --> 00:04:08,358
Yes.

67
00:04:08,638 --> 00:04:10,654
Holding or displaying paper?

68
00:04:10,934 --> 00:04:12,806
Sometimes.

69
00:04:13,086 --> 00:04:15,102
Cutting or modifying paper?

70
00:04:15,382 --> 00:04:17,254
Sometimes.

71
00:04:17,534 --> 00:04:21,686
Then: primarily used for fastening or attaching things together?

72
00:04:21,966 --> 00:04:23,838
Yes.

73
00:04:24,118 --> 00:04:25,990
And now it guesses.

74
00:04:26,270 --> 00:04:28,142
Stapler.

75
00:04:28,422 --> 00:04:30,294
I press Correct.

76
00:04:30,574 --> 00:04:35,086
The app says: GPT read your mind in seventeen questions.

77
00:04:35,886 --> 00:04:43,542
The guesser starts well, with questions that cut the field in half, living or not, man-made or not, big or small.

78
00:04:43,822 --> 00:04:45,694
But in the middle it wandered.

79
00:04:45,974 --> 00:04:57,110
Within a few turns it asked about personal care, entertainment and cooking, when a better question would have used what it already knew: not electronic, used for work, found in offices.

80
00:04:57,390 --> 00:05:01,014
It still got there, but it used seventeen questions of twenty.

81
00:05:01,294 --> 00:05:05,638
I'd expect a good human player to need fewer, though I haven't measured that.

82
00:05:05,918 --> 00:05:09,446
And the host side showed something else: the Sometimes answer.

83
00:05:09,726 --> 00:05:13,398
Real questions are often vague, and the host has to choose.

84
00:05:14,198 --> 00:05:21,014
This was one game in each direction, so it says nothing about how good GPT is at twenty questions in general.

85
00:05:21,294 --> 00:05:29,262
In the guessing game I picked the object myself, a stapler, which is a common thing, and my Sometimes answers were my own judgments.

86
00:05:29,542 --> 00:05:35,590
In the host game, the animal list is short and I wrote it, so it's easier for me than for a new player.

87
00:05:35,870 --> 00:05:41,438
The host is also just a language model: it can answer wrongly, and the app has no way to check.

88
00:05:41,718 --> 00:05:46,782
I built this with Claude Code, and I read and ran the code, but read it yourself too.

89
00:05:47,582 --> 00:05:53,174
Next I'd add a scoreboard, harder categories, and a mode where the host also has to give a hint.

90
00:05:53,454 --> 00:05:58,782
And I'd measure how many questions GPT needs across many objects, rather than one.

91
00:05:59,062 --> 00:06:03,982
The code is in the description, with no key needed beyond your own Azure setup.

92
00:06:04,262 --> 00:06:09,638
If you enjoyed this, follow for more AI and ML builds, and tell me what I should think of next.
